Here Ce2 and C 2 are two copies of the complex plane. The well-known Jacobian
Conjecture states that such f is polynomially invertible (see surveys in [1], [2].)
The mapping f satisfying (0.1) is polynomially invertible if and only if a generic
point in C 2 has one preimage. The topological degree of a mapping is said to
be the number of preimages of a generic point. It is known that there are no
polynomial mappings of degree two or three satisfying (0.1). The case of degree
two is elementary and the case of degree three was considered in [3].
